            <div class="prose max-w-none" x-show="lang === 'en'" x-cloak>
                <h2>New Changes Observed This Week</h2>
<p>This week, after communicating with several teams managing 5+ ad accounts, we noticed a shift from "managing by intuition" to "managing by logic" in account layering. Many teams previously struggled with multi-account chaos—scattered top-ups triggering balance warnings, creative assets tested across accounts without effect tracking, and discovering complete exposure blackouts for certain product lines only after account bans.</p>
<p>The core observation: more teams are reorganizing account structures using three-layer logic.</p>
<h2>Layering by Product Line: Common Yet Problematic</h2>
<p>Teams layering by product line typically structure as: one app paired with 2-3 accounts, each running different regions or creative directions. While this provides clear data isolation, it fragments top-up management.</p>
<p>A typical case this week: a utility app team had 6 accounts covering North America, Europe, and Southeast Asia, with 2 accounts per market (one for Google, one for Meta). The problem emerged during end-of-quarter platform-wide credit limits when 3 accounts triggered balance warnings simultaneously. During financial reconciliation, they discovered a 2-day delay between one account's top-up records and spend data.</p>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: For product-line layered teams, implement "primary account aggregation" in the top-up process—maintain one central account per product line for unified top-ups, with sub-accounts retaining minimum balances and auto-transfer triggers on warnings.</p>
<h2>Layering by Campaign Stage: Ideal for Iterative Teams</h2>
<p>The second approach layers by creative and campaign stage. One account handles "new creative testing," another "scaling proven creatives," and a third "conversion harvesting." This is common among e-commerce and gaming teams with rapid creative iteration.</p>
<p>A gaming overseas team shared their layering strategy:</p>
<ul>
<li>Test Account A: 20+ new creatives weekly, fixed $500/day budget</li>
<li>Scale Account B:承接 A 中 CTR &gt; 2% creatives, weekly budget increase</li>
<li>Harvest Account C: ROI &gt; 1.5 proven creatives only, capped budget</li>
</ul>
<p>This structure reduced their CPA by 18% in three months, as creative lifecycle management became trackable.</p>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Stage-layered teams need clear "creative promotion rules"—what metrics qualify for scaling, what conversion data supports harvesting decisions. Use independent tracking sheets or tools for status marking rather than relying on memory.</p>
<h2>Layering by Platform: Clear Structure but Internal Synergy Overlooked</h2>
<p>Platform-based layering is simplest: one platform, one account system. Benefits include operator focus on single platform rules and good risk isolation. The challenge: when the same product needs to scale on both TikTok and Meta, two account teams may use identical creatives, reaching overlapping audiences and creating internal competition.</p>
<p>An emerging observation: some teams are attempting "cross-platform coordination layering"—primary accounts build volume on one platform, auxiliary accounts capture overflow brand search traffic on others. This requires strong communication mechanisms between teams.</p>
<p><strong>Recommendation</strong>: Platform-layered teams should conduct monthly cross-platform creative and audience overlap analysis to avoid internal waste.</p>
<h2>Weekly Summary</h2>
<p>No standard answer exists for multi-account layering. The key is choosing an approach suited to team size, product category, and media mix. Regardless of layering logic, three elements must be solidified in advance: top-up processes, creative tracking, and risk protocols. More accounts mean higher risk without structure.</p>
